      <header class="cod-title-block">
        <p class="cod-chapter-label">Chapter Ten · Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits?</p>
        <h1 class="cod-title">The Sermon</h1>
        <p class="cod-subtitle">One Voice and a Silent Room</p>
        <p class="cod-tagline">Every church has a sermon. The apostles had a conversation. Somewhere in between, the flock was kept on milk.</p>
      </header>

      <p class="cod-pullquote">Spirit of Truth or Seducing Spirits? Let Scripture and history decide.</p>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">INTRODUCTION</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Question No One Is Asking</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Every Sunday, in every kind of church, one man walks up to a raised platform and stands behind a pulpit. For thirty, forty, or sixty minutes he speaks, and everyone else sits in rows facing him and listens. No one asks a question. No one answers him. When he finishes, there is a closing song, and everyone goes home. The next week, the same man does it again.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture commands preaching. Paul told Timothy, "Preach the word; be instant in season, out of season" (2 Timothy 4:2), and asked, "how shall they hear without a preacher?" (Romans 10:14). This chapter is about the <strong>form</strong>: one voice, every week, to the same silent room, which never answers and is never asked to test what it heard.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">In the altar call, the enemy erased the blood. In the ministry system, he erased the walk. <strong>In the sermon, he kept the believer on milk.</strong> He was fed every week, and he never grew. Scripture names the power behind a practice like that: "in the latter times some shall depart from the faith, giving heed to seducing spirits" (1 Timothy 4:1). Against it stands the Spirit of truth, who "will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13).</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">I</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Preaching Was</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In the New Testament, "preach" is usually the Greek <em>kēryssō</em>: to herald, to announce like a town crier. Philip "preached Christ" in a city of Samaria (Acts 8:5). Paul disputed "in the market daily with them that met with him" (Acts 17:17), and spent two years in Rome "preaching the kingdom of God" to all who came to his house (Acts 28:30-31). Preaching was the announcement of the good news to people who had not yet heard it.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Inside the assembly, the pattern was the one the Law had always kept: the Word read aloud, then explained. When Ezra gathered the people, the Levites "read in the book in the law of God distinctly, and gave the sense, and caused them to understand the reading" (Nehemiah 8:8). In the synagogue at Nazareth, Yahushua stood up, read from Isaiah, sat down, and spoke (Luke 4:16-21). In the synagogue at Antioch, after "the reading of the law and the prophets," the rulers asked the visitors, "if ye have any word of exhortation for the people, say on" (Acts 13:15). Paul gave Timothy the same order: "give attendance to reading, to exhortation, to doctrine" (1 Timothy 4:13).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">When the believers met together, more than one spoke. "When ye come together, every one of you hath a psalm, hath a doctrine, hath a tongue, hath a revelation, hath an interpretation … Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge … For ye may all prophesy one by one, that all may learn, and all may be comforted" (1 Corinthians 14:26, 29, 31). They were "teaching and admonishing one another" (Colossians 3:16) and "exhorting one another" (Hebrews 10:25).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">At Troas, "Paul preached unto them" until midnight, and a young man fell asleep and fell from the window (Acts 20:7-9). The Greek word there is <em>dialegomai</em> — to reason, to discuss, the root of the word <em>dialogue</em>. After the young man was raised, Paul "talked a long while, even till break of day" (Acts 20:11). That word is <em>homileō</em>, to converse.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Bereans were called noble because they "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" — and that was about the preaching of Paul himself (Acts 17:11). Every hearer was a tester, and every tester was expected to go and do. That is the pattern of the Spirit of truth.</p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">II</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Schools of Persuasion (A.D. 300-500)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">In the Greek and Roman world, rhetoric — the art of moving a crowd with speech — was the highest education a man could get. It had its own schools, teachers, and contests. A skilled orator could hold thousands in a theater and be applauded like a performer.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Two of the most famous preachers of the early church came out of that world. Augustine was a professor of rhetoric in Milan before he became a bishop. John of Antioch studied under Libanius, a leading pagan teacher of rhetoric, and later generations called him <em>Chrysostom</em> — "Golden Mouth." In Antioch and Constantinople, the crowds clapped and cheered at his sermons, and he scolded them for treating the church like a theater.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">When Constantine gave the church its first great halls, they were built on the pattern of the Roman basilica — the public hall where a magistrate sat on a raised platform at one end and spoke to the people below. The bishop's chair, the <em>cathedra</em>, took the magistrate's place. The word <em>cathedral</em> means the building where the bishop's chair is.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul refused that art at Corinth: "I came not with excellency of speech or of wisdom … my speech and my preaching was not with enticing words of man's wisdom, but in demonstration of the Spirit and of power: that your faith should not stand in the wisdom of men, but in the power of God" (1 Corinthians 2:1, 4-5). He warned the Colossians, "lest any man should beguile you with enticing words," and "spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men" (Colossians 2:4, 8). <strong>The art Paul refused became the way the church spoke every week.</strong> A seducing spirit does not need a false message. It only needs a hearer moved by the speaker instead of by the Word.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Two Orators in the Pulpit: Augustine and Chrysostom</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">Augustine and John Chrysostom were the two most famous preachers of the early church, and both were trained speakers before they were preachers. They lived at the same time, on opposite sides of the Roman world. Augustine was born in 354 at Thagaste in North Africa. John was born around 349 at Antioch in Syria. They never met.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>Augustine, the professor.</strong> Augustine studied rhetoric at Carthage and taught it there, then in Rome. In 384 he was appointed the official teacher of rhetoric in Milan, a post that carried the job of giving public speeches in praise of the emperor. In his <em>Confessions</em>, he says why he first went to hear Ambrose, the bishop of Milan, preach: not to learn what Ambrose said, but to judge how well he said it — whether his eloquence matched his fame. The speaking drew him first, and the message came after. Ambrose baptized him at Easter in 387. Augustine became a priest at Hippo in 391 and its bishop a few years later.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Near the end of his life, around 426, Augustine finished a handbook for Christian teachers called <em>On Christian Doctrine</em>. Its fourth book is a manual for preachers, and it is built on the Roman orator Cicero. Cicero taught that a speaker must do three things: teach, delight, and move his hearers. Augustine carried those three aims into the pulpit, and for a thousand years after him, the preacher's craft was taught as a branch of the orator's.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>John, the Golden Mouth.</strong> At Antioch, John studied under Libanius, the most famous pagan teacher of rhetoric of his day. The church historian Sozomen records that when Libanius was dying and was asked who should take his place, he answered, "John, if the Christians had not stolen him." John was ordained a priest at Antioch in 386 and preached there for twelve years. In 387, when riots broke out over a new tax and the crowds tore down the statues of the emperor, the city waited in fear of punishment, and John preached a series of sermons to it through those weeks. They made his name.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">His congregations clapped and cheered when he spoke well, as crowds did in the theater. He rebuked them for it more than once, telling them that the church was not a theater and that praise for the speaker did nothing if they went home unchanged. According to one account, when he preached a sermon against applause, the crowd applauded it.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In 398 John was brought to Constantinople, the capital, and made its bishop. His preaching against the luxury of the court turned the empress Eudoxia against him. He was deposed by a council of bishops in 403, recalled, and sent into exile again in 404. He died in 407, still being marched to a more distant place of banishment.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The two men are honored as saints and teachers of the church. The record shows something else as well: the pulpit of the fourth century was filled by men trained for the stage of public speaking, and they brought its methods, its standards, and its applause with them.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">III</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Raised Pulpit (1500s-1700s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">For a thousand years the Mass stood at the center of the service, and the sermon was short or missing. The Reformation brought the sermon back and made it the center of everything.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">On January 1, 1519, Ulrich Zwingli began preaching straight through the Gospel of Matthew in Zurich. Luther, Calvin, and the Reformers after them made the preached Word the heart of Protestant worship. The form they kept was the one they inherited: one ordained man, raised up in a pulpit, speaking to a congregation seated in rows. Protestant churches moved the pulpit to the middle of the front wall, high above the people. The Puritans of England and New England set hourglasses on their pulpits to time sermons that often ran an hour or two. The people's part was to sit, listen, and take notes.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul told the assembly "ye may all prophesy one by one" and "let the other judge" (1 Corinthians 14:29, 31). The Reformation gave the people a Bible in their own language and then sat them down in silence to hear one man explain it. <strong>The Word was restored to the pulpit and taken out of the people's mouths.</strong> This is the work of a seducing spirit: the Word was in the room, and the people were kept from handling it.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — The Prophesyings and the Hourglass</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">The Reformers did not only preach often. They preached at a pace the church had never seen. John Calvin preached in Geneva most weekdays as well as on Sundays. From 1549 the city paid a man named Denis Raguenier to write his sermons down as he spoke, and more than two thousand of them were recorded in the years that followed. In Geneva, attending the sermon was required by law.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The prophesyings.</strong> In Elizabethan England, a practice grew up that, by name at least, looked back to 1 Corinthians 14. Groups of ministers met in market towns for what they called "prophesyings." One minister would preach on a passage, then a second and third would speak on the same text, and the others would judge what had been said. Lay people were allowed to come and listen. It was the closest thing in that age to "let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge" — but only the clergy spoke.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Queen Elizabeth saw the meetings as a threat to order and ordered them stopped. In 1576 Edmund Grindal, the Archbishop of Canterbury, refused. He wrote to the queen that he could not in conscience suppress them. In 1577 she suspended him from his office, and the prophesyings were put down. Even the clergy were not to weigh one another's words in public.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The art of preaching.</strong> In 1592 the Cambridge Puritan William Perkins published <em>The Art of Prophesying</em>, one of the first preaching manuals of the English Reformation. In his use, "prophesying" meant preaching: the one ordained man explaining a text and applying it to the hearers. The word from 1 Corinthians 14, where "ye may all prophesy one by one," now named the work of one man in a pulpit.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>The hourglass.</strong> In English parish churches of the 1500s and 1600s, an hourglass often stood in an iron stand beside the pulpit, and some of those stands survive. A sermon was expected to last at least an hour. A preacher who turned the glass over to begin a second hour was showing his zeal. Through all of it, the congregation sat below the pulpit, silent, and was taught to take notes.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The Reformation opened the Bible to the people. The record of these years shows how much of the old pattern stayed in place: the speaking belonged to the ordained, the weighing of words was taken away even from them, and the people's part was to listen.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">IV</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The American Pulpit (1700s-1900s)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">George Whitefield, the great preacher of the First Great Awakening, had acted in plays as a schoolboy, and he preached with a stage actor's voice and gestures to open-air crowds sometimes numbering in the tens of thousands. The revival preachers who followed built sermons to move a crowd, and the altar call of Chapter Six became the closing scene.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Seminaries taught <em>homiletics</em>, the craft of building and delivering sermons, as a professional skill. Churches chose the pastor by his preaching, judged him by it, and paid him for it. In the twentieth century, radio carried one preacher's voice to millions who would never speak a word back.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah told Ezekiel, "they come unto thee as the people cometh, and they sit before thee as my people, and they hear thy words, but they will not do them … lo, thou art unto them as a very lovely song of one that hath a pleasant voice, and can play well on an instrument: for they hear thy words, but they do them not" (Ezekiel 33:31-32). <strong>A sermon enjoyed like a song and never obeyed is the seducing spirit's favorite kind.</strong> It costs the hearer nothing, and it lets him feel he has been with Yahuah.</p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — Whitefield, the Actor in the Field</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">George Whitefield was born in 1714 at the Bell Inn in Gloucester, England, where his family kept the tavern. As a schoolboy he loved the stage and acted in school plays, and he later wrote that he had been fond of reading plays and acting them. At Oxford he joined the Holy Club with John and Charles Wesley.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">In 1739, when parish pulpits were closed to him, he began preaching in the open air, first to coal miners near Bristol. The crowds grew into the thousands and then the tens of thousands. He crossed the Atlantic seven times and preached from Georgia to New England.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">Benjamin Franklin heard him in Philadelphia. In his <em>Autobiography</em>, Franklin tells how he walked backward away from Whitefield down the street, measuring how far the voice carried, and worked out that more than thirty thousand people could have heard him at once. Franklin also noticed that Whitefield's sermons improved as he repeated them from town to town, until every word, pause, and gesture was perfected — the way an actor polishes a part.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The famous actor David Garrick is reported to have said he would give a hundred guineas to be able to say "Oh!" the way Whitefield said it. Whitefield wept, shouted, acted out the scenes he preached on, and held his crowds with his voice.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">He was sincere, and many dated their turning to Yahuah from his preaching. The record also shows what America learned from him: that a single gifted voice could move a crowd of thousands, and that the preacher who moved the crowd best drew the most. The revival preachers who came after him built on that lesson.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">V</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Screen (1950s-Today)</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Television made the preacher a star. Many large churches now run several campuses at once, and at most of them the pastor is not in the room; his sermon plays on a giant screen, live or recorded. Sermons are released as podcasts and video series with titles, graphics, and matching social media posts. Companies sell ready-made sermon series, complete with outlines and artwork.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The room has stayed silent. Ten thousand people can hear a sermon on Sunday and not one of them can ask the preacher a question or open the scroll in front of the others to see "whether those things were so." Paul warned, "they will not endure sound doctrine; but after their own lusts shall they heap to themselves teachers, having itching ears" (2 Timothy 4:3). Isaiah heard the people say, "Prophesy not unto us right things, speak unto us smooth things" (Isaiah 30:10). Jeremiah recorded Yahuah's answer: "the prophets prophesy falsely … and my people love to have it so" (Jeremiah 5:31). The Spirit of truth sends a believer into the Word. A seducing spirit sends him to a screen to have the Word handed to him.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>A conversation became a speech. The speech went up into a raised chair. The chair became a pulpit, the pulpit became a stage, and the stage became a screen. And the room never spoke again.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Scripture Shows Instead</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">This is what the Spirit of truth produces in an assembly.</p>

      <ul class="cod-list">
        <li><strong>Preach to those who have not heard.</strong> Proclaim the good news in the open, as Philip and Paul did (Acts 8:5; 17:17).</li>
        <li><strong>Read the Word aloud in the assembly, and give the sense of it</strong> (Nehemiah 8:8; 1 Timothy 4:13).</li>
        <li><strong>Let more than one speak, and let the others judge.</strong> "Let the prophets speak two or three, and let the other judge" (1 Corinthians 14:29).</li>
        <li><strong>Reason together.</strong> Ask, answer, and search the Scriptures to see "whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11; 20:7).</li>
        <li><strong>Teach one another, and teach at home.</strong> "Teaching and admonishing one another" (Colossians 3:16), and the father at his own table (Deuteronomy 6:7).</li>
        <li><strong>Grow past the milk.</strong> Strong meat belongs to those whose senses are "exercised" by use (Hebrews 5:14). "Grow in grace, and in the knowledge" of Yahushua Messiah (2 Peter 3:18).</li>
        <li><strong>Call no man master.</strong> "One is your Master, even Messiah; and all ye are brethren" (Matthew 23:8). Elders serve as examples, not as lords (1 Peter 5:3).</li>
        <li><strong>Then go and do it.</strong> "Be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves" (James 1:22).</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part"><p class="cod-part-label">VI</p><h2 class="cod-part-title">One Voice</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The pulpit did more than raise the speaker. It made him the only speaker. The same man stands in the same place, week after week, and tells the same people what the Scriptures mean. No one answers him, and no one else is asked.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The Reformation did not remove the priest; it renamed him. Calvin's church order for Geneva in 1541 set out four standing offices, and the first was the pastor, the man ordained to preach. The Council of Trent ordered seminaries in 1563 to train priests, and the Protestant churches built their own. The first graduate seminary in America opened at Andover, Massachusetts, in 1808. From then on, a young man went away to be trained by professors, came back with a degree, and was set over a congregation to speak for it. <strong>The pastor is the priest's grandson.</strong> He holds the same place: one ordained man between the people and the Word.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture leaves no room for that place. Yahuah told Israel at Sinai, "ye shall be unto me a kingdom of priests" (Exodus 19:6). Peter wrote the same to every believer: "Ye also, as lively stones, are built up a spiritual house, an holy priesthood … ye are a chosen generation, a royal priesthood" (1 Peter 2:5, 9). John wrote that Yahushua "hath made us kings and priests unto God and his Father" (Revelation 1:6). Under the Law there was one high priest over the priests. Under the new covenant there is still one: "we have a great high priest, that is passed into the heavens, Yahushua the Son of God" (Hebrews 4:14), who "hath an unchangeable priesthood" (Hebrews 7:24). That seat is taken. Every believer is a priest, and no believer-priest stands above another. <strong>To set one man over the rest is to put a man in the High Priest's seat.</strong></p>

      <p class="cod-body">In the whole King James New Testament, the word <em>pastors</em> appears once: "he gave some, apostles; and some, prophets; and some, evangelists; and some, pastors and teachers" (Ephesians 4:11). The Greek word is <em>poimēn</em>, a shepherd, and it is a serving word. Yahushua said, "whosoever of you will be the chiefest, shall be servant of all" (Mark 10:44). Peter, an elder himself, told the elders to feed the flock "neither as being lords over God's heritage, but being ensamples to the flock" (1 Peter 5:3). Scripture names the Shepherd over the flock: "that great shepherd of the sheep" (Hebrews 13:20), "the chief Shepherd" (1 Peter 5:4), "one fold, and one shepherd" (John 10:16). A lowly serving task became the head of the church.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahushua forbade it: "But be not ye called Rabbi: for one is your Master, even Messiah; and all ye are brethren … Neither be ye called masters: for one is your Master, even Messiah" (Matthew 23:8, 10). John wrote that the anointing "abideth in you, and ye need not that any man teach you" (1 John 2:27). And John named the man in the assembly who wanted the one voice: "Diotrephes, who loveth to have the preeminence among them, receiveth us not … and casteth them out of the church" (3 John 9-10).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Israel asked for one man once before. "Make us a king to judge us like all the nations," they told Samuel, and Yahuah answered, "they have not rejected thee, but they have rejected me, that I should not reign over them" (1 Samuel 8:5, 7). The pulpit gives the same comfort. The pastor studies, so the people do not have to. The pastor decides what the verse means, so the people do not have to.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Chapter Seven traced the same pattern in Children's Church: one approved voice, heard over and over, in a room where no one answers back, until it is the voice the hearer thinks with. A believer who has heard one man explain the Scriptures every week for thirty years hears that explanation before he finishes reading the verse.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Scripture says, "smite the shepherd, and the sheep shall be scattered" (Zechariah 13:7). <strong>This is a seducing spirit at work. The enemy does not need to strike the shepherd. He only needs to hold the one voice the whole flock listens to.</strong> Turn one man, and he turns a thousand. And one voice is one bottle: the flock drinks only what that man pours, and no one in the room is trained to find the meat for himself.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>One Shepherd. One High Priest. One Master. All ye are brethren.</strong></p>

      <details class="cod-deepdive">
        <summary>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-label">
            <span class="cod-deepdive-icon">✦</span>
            <span class="cod-deepdive-title">Go Deeper — How the Seminary Was Born</span>
          </span>
          <span class="cod-deepdive-chevron">▾</span>
        </summary>
        <div class="cod-deepdive-body">
          <p class="cod-body">For most of church history, there was no seminary. Priests were trained in cathedral schools, in monasteries, or in the universities that grew up in the Middle Ages. The seminary as a separate school for training clergy was created by the Council of Trent. On July 15, 1563, the council ordered every diocese to set up a college where boys and young men would be raised and trained for the priesthood, apart from the world.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>In America.</strong> In colonial America, Protestant ministers were usually trained at Harvard or Yale and then lived and studied for a time in the home of an older pastor, reading his books and watching him work. That changed after 1805, when Harvard appointed Henry Ware, a Unitarian, to its chair of divinity. Orthodox Congregationalists, alarmed, founded their own school to train ministers. Andover Theological Seminary opened in Massachusetts in 1808 as the first graduate seminary in the country. Princeton Theological Seminary followed in 1812 for the Presbyterians, and within a few decades every major denomination had its own.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>What the seminary taught.</strong> Andover set up a professorship of sacred rhetoric, and preaching was taught as a craft with its own textbooks, rules, and practice sermons. In 1871 Yale founded the Lyman Beecher Lectures on Preaching, still given every year. The seminary student learned Greek, Hebrew, and doctrine — and he learned to deliver a sermon.</p>
          <p class="cod-body"><strong>What it produced.</strong> The seminary made the pastor a professional. A man was now prepared for the pulpit by a school, approved by his denomination, and called by a congregation that expected him to be its trained voice. The people in the pews had no such training, and were not expected to have it. The distance between the one who speaks and those who listen was no longer only in the height of the pulpit. It was written on a diploma.</p>
          <p class="cod-body">The first assemblies had elders chosen from among themselves, men known for how they lived (1 Timothy 3:1-7; Titus 1:5-9). Paul's list of what an elder must be says nothing about a school. It speaks of a man "apt to teach" whose household and life were in order.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">What Is the Harm?</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">Paul said the fight is "against principalities, against powers" (Ephesians 6:12). The harm here is one many believers have felt without having a name for it.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">A believer can go to church three times a week for thirty years, hear thousands of sermons, and then open the Scriptures for himself and find that most of what he was given was milk. Hebrews describes him: "when for the time ye ought to be teachers, ye have need that one teach you again which be the first principles of the oracles of God; and are become such as have need of milk, and not of strong meat … But strong meat belongeth to them that are of full age, even those who by reason of use have their senses exercised to discern both good and evil" (Hebrews 5:12-14). Paul said the same to the Corinthians: "I have fed you with milk, and not with meat: for hitherto ye were not able to bear it, neither yet now are ye able" (1 Corinthians 3:2).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Milk is food for a newborn, given "that ye may grow thereby" (1 Peter 2:2). Strong meat belongs to those who exercise their own senses "by reason of use." The silent room never allows that use. The hearer does not search; he receives. He does not test; he trusts. He does not speak; he listens, and always to the same voice. And so he stays a child, fed by someone else. The Spirit of truth was sent to guide each believer "into all truth" (John 16:13). A seducing spirit is content to let one man do the guiding for all of them.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Yahuah spoke through Ezekiel against the shepherds who kept the flock that way: "Woe be to the shepherds of Israel that do feed themselves! should not the shepherds feed the flocks?" (Ezekiel 34:2). "Seemeth it a small thing unto you to have eaten up the good pasture, but ye must tread down with your feet the residue of your pastures? … And as for my flock, they eat that which ye have trodden with your feet" (34:18-19). Then He promised, "I will feed my flock, and I will cause them to lie down … I will feed them in a good pasture" (34:14-15). "He maketh me to lie down in green pastures" (Psalm 23:2).</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The milk never asks anything of the one who drinks it. James warned that hearers who are not doers are "deceiving your own selves" (James 1:22). A believer can hear Scripture preached for a lifetime and never once be asked whether he did any of it.</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>The enemy does not need to stop the preaching. He only needs the flock to keep coming back for milk.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-divider">◆</div>

      <section class="cod-part cod-part--plain"><h2 class="cod-part-title">The Verdict</h2><div class="cod-part-rule"></div></section>

      <p class="cod-body">The sermon is not a doctrine anyone defends. It is a practice, and every church has one, so no one checks it. Every church says the Word must be obeyed. Then it seats the people in silence, gives them one voice to listen to for the rest of their lives, and sends them home.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">The record runs in one direction. A conversation in Troas, where Paul reasoned and talked until dawn. Then the rhetoric schools, the applause in Antioch, and a bishop's chair set where the magistrate sat. Then the raised pulpit and the hourglass, the priest renamed the pastor and trained in a seminary, the actor's voice in the open field, the radio, and the screen. Every step made the speaker louder and the hearer quieter, and every step came after the apostles. That is the direction a seducing spirit always moves: the speaker up, the hearer down, the Word out of reach. In the altar call the enemy erased the blood. In the ministry system he erased the walk. In the sermon he kept the believer on milk.</p>

      <p class="cod-body">Revelation was written to be read aloud in the assembly: "Blessed is he that readeth, and they that hear the words of this prophecy, and keep those things which are written therein" (Revelation 1:3). At its close the Master says it again: "Behold, I come quickly: blessed is he that keepeth the sayings of the prophecy of this book" (Revelation 22:7). The blessing is on the one who keeps. And the One who feeds the flock at the end is the Shepherd Himself: "the Lamb which is in the midst of the throne shall feed them, and shall lead them unto living fountains of waters" (Revelation 7:17).</p>

      <p class="cod-pullquote"><strong>Leave the milk. Search the Word for yourself. Feed on the meat. Then do it.</strong></p>

      <div class="cod-end"><span class="cod-end-mark">◆</span></div>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

